% ============================================================================
% kingdoms — each biological kingdom of life mapped to every example organism
% the source lists for it (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first ideas a biology learner meets: every living thing on
% Earth is sorted into a small number of top-level KINGDOMS, and each kingdom
% has familiar example organisms that live in it. "Animals are in Animalia;
% flowering plants are in Plantae; mushrooms are Fungi; amoebas are Protista;
% cyanobacteria are Bacteria." Before you can reason about an organism at all,
% it helps to know WHICH kingdom it belongs to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? kingdom_example(fungi, $Example)      % mushrooms ; yeast ; molds
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `kingdom_example(kingdom, example)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns EVERY
% example WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these kingdoms (it never invents an example). Each
% kingdom is deliberately ONE-TO-MANY: the source names several example
% organisms per kingdom, so `kingdom_example(animalia, $Example)` binds
% `$Example` to EACH of them in turn, just like `flame_color.adj`'s many-to-one
% `red` (strontium ; lithium) already established the reverse shape in this
% same stdlib.
%
% Each kingdom below and EVERY example the source lists for it, as a truth
% table:
%
%     kingdom     example                a beginner's cue
%     ---------   --------------------   ----------------------------------
%     animalia    humans                 the animal kingdom
%     animalia    birds
%     animalia    crustaceans
%     animalia    sponges
%     plantae     flowers                the plant kingdom
%     plantae     grasses
%     plantae     conifers
%     plantae     multicellular_algae
%     plantae     ferns
%     plantae     mosses
%     fungi       mushrooms              the fungus kingdom
%     fungi       yeast
%     fungi       molds
%     protista    amoebas                single-celled eukaryotes
%     protista    diatoms
%     protista    dinoflagellates
%     protista    ciliates
%     protista    slime_molds
%     protista    single_celled_algae
%     bacteria    cyanobacteria          the prokaryote kingdom
%     bacteria    gram_positive_bacteria
%     bacteria    gram_negative_bacteria
%     bacteria    actinobacteria
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an example and recall its kingdom:
%
%     ? kingdom_example($Kingdom, mushrooms)   % fungi
%
% A word that is NOT one of these kingdoms — `virus`, `mineral`, `rock` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a kingdom recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing an example. (Viruses are a classic example: they are not placed in
% any of these kingdoms, so the table honestly abstains on `virus`.) That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the kingdom→example pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `example` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase snake_case atom, chosen
% to be an organism the source EXPLICITLY lists for that kingdom — never one the
% source does not support. (Where the source's own phrase is two or more words
% — "multicellular algae", "slime molds", "single-celled algae", "Gram-positive
% ... bacteria" — the atom joins them with underscores, so every value stays
% literally checkable against the quoted "Examples:" span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every kingdom→example pair
% below was read out of a single fetched Science Notes "Kingdoms of Life in
% Biology" page, and any pair whose example could not be verified there would
% have been dropped). Each pair, with the verbatim "Examples:" span that states
% it (all from the one source below):
%
%   animalia → humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges
%     "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges"
%   plantae → flowers, grasses, conifers, multicellular_algae, ferns, mosses
%     "Examples: Flowers, grasses, conifers, multicellular algae, ferns, mosses"
%   fungi → mushrooms, yeast, molds
%     "Examples: Mushrooms, yeast, molds"
%   protista → amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime_molds,
%   single_celled_algae
%     "Examples: Amoebas, diatoms, dinoflagellates, ciliates, slime molds,
%      single-celled algae"
%   bacteria → cyanobacteria, gram_positive_bacteria, gram_negative_bacteria,
%   actinobacteria
%     "Examples: Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, cyanobacteria,
%      actinobacteria" (the "and"-joined pair names two distinct, literal
%      examples in the same list -- not an "or"-joined alternative
%      categorization -- so each is its own row)
%
% All five spans are from the same page,
% https://sciencenotes.org/kingdoms-of-life-in-biology/ — a secondary science
% teaching resource, so `trust consensus` (not a primary .gov/.edu source).
%
% EXTENDED this cycle from 5 to 23 rows: the SAME already-cited Science Notes
% page's own "Examples:" lines name eighteen MORE organisms beyond the five
% originally shipped (one per kingdom) — zero new source page needed,
% WebFetch-verified (re-fetched the live page and confirmed every "Examples:"
% line word-for-word against the header's original transcription) before
% writing. This is the fifth successful extend-pattern win in this loop's
% recent run (after animal-classes.adj, idiom-meaning.adj, plant-tropisms.adj,
% simile-meaning.adj), and the first to extend a table from single-valued to
% many-valued per key rather than adding new keys — the same reverse-lookup
% shape `flame_color.adj` already established (many values recalled for one
% bound argument), just applied in the OTHER direction (many `example` values
% recalled for one bound `kingdom`).
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the "Examples:" line that fixes
% the first row (animalia → humans). Every OTHER row's verbatim "Examples:" span
% is listed above, so each example remains independently checkable.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table kingdom_example {
    columns kingdom, example

    row (animalia, humans)
    row (animalia, birds)
    row (animalia, crustaceans)
    row (animalia, sponges)
    row (plantae, flowers)
    row (plantae, grasses)
    row (plantae, conifers)
    row (plantae, multicellular_algae)
    row (plantae, ferns)
    row (plantae, mosses)
    row (fungi, mushrooms)
    row (fungi, yeast)
    row (fungi, molds)
    row (protista, amoebas)
    row (protista, diatoms)
    row (protista, dinoflagellates)
    row (protista, ciliates)
    row (protista, slime_molds)
    row (protista, single_celled_algae)
    row (bacteria, cyanobacteria)
    row (bacteria, gram_positive_bacteria)
    row (bacteria, gram_negative_bacteria)
    row (bacteria, actinobacteria)

    source "Examples: Humans, birds, crustaceans, sponges"
    locator "https://sciencenotes.org/kingdoms-of-life-in-biology/"
    trust consensus
}
